From d779b3a497e3b79b9e1e7aac9b438d8ccce170c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 3 <phase3@openwch.local>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 03:48:55 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] RISC-V: match WCH XW gate diagnostic bytes

XW-disabled compressed instructions and the XW/ZCD conflict reported
different GAS diagnostics from WCH even though their success paths and
encodings matched.

Reset the normal operand diagnostic before WCH candidate rejection and
keep the XW/ZCD conflict in GAS, where the existing sticky initial-XW
state can be honored; the shared BFD subset query keeps its upstream
shape.  Preserve the unrecognized/missing C-or-Zca state for XW candidates
while current RVC assembly is disabled.  Make the DejaGNU expectations
assert the official text and RVC state transitions.

Observed symptom: with compressed assembly disabled while XW is enabled,
which is the ordinary EVT configuration of -march=rv32imac_xw plus a
source-level .option norvc, every XW mnemonic reported "illegal operands"
where WCH reports "unrecognized opcode", and the four sp forms
additionally lost the whole ", extension ... required" clause because they
have no later candidate to refill it.  The gate below therefore does not
test the initial XW eligibility at all: compressed assembly being off is
by itself enough to reject an XW candidate, in the same shape as the
ordinary extension gate rejecting an unsupported class.  The same defect
is reachable with malformed operands, and through an ELF .attribute arch
instead of -march since both establish the eligibility through
riscv_set_arch, so the new DejaGNU cases cover all four combinations.

Three properties of the two XW gates are worth recording, because a later
reader can otherwise undo this by accident:

  * The two gates partition the XW state space and neither is redundant.
    With compressed assembly off, the gate before the diagnostic reset
    rejects every XW candidate; with it on, the gate after the reset
    rejects them when the initial architecture never enabled XW; with both
    on, the private encoding is assembled.  The comment in the source
    carries the same table.

  * The first gate reports INSN_CLASS_ZCA rather than the candidate's own
    INSN_CLASS_XW.  That is the one substantive asymmetry with the
    ordinary upstream gate and it is deliberate: every XW encoding is 16
    bits wide, so the extension a user has to add to make the line
    assemble is C or Zca, which is also the extension WCH names.

  * Rejecting XW candidates this early is safe only because the opcode
    table lists the XW entry of a mnemonic ahead of its non-XW entries, so
    a later candidate of the same mnemonic still overwrites the extension
    recorded here, and because every INSN_CLASS_XW entry encodes in two
    bytes, so the existing instruction length check would have rejected it
    a few lines further down anyway.

Teaching riscv_multi_subset_supports about INSN_CLASS_XW instead is not an
option: it decides from the current subset list, while WCH keys these
private encodings on the initial architecture, which .option arch does not
change.

Behavior specification: direct WCH 15.2.0 assembler probes recorded for
phase-3.1, including the RVC on/off and sticky-XW neighbor matrices, and
  analysis/toolchain/phase3-review.md P2-2
The expectations of the new and extended test cases were captured from the
official WCH assembler on exactly these inputs.  Encoding and gate
behavior remain sourced from
  analysis/toolchain/wch-gcc-toolchain-survey.md sections 5.3 and 5.4
